# T. PROVIDER AUTHORITY AND EXECUTION PATH

## PM-PROVIDER-001 — Provider Trust Lifecycle

Portable states include `DISCOVERED`, `AUTHENTICATED_UNADMITTED`, `ADMITTED_FOR_SCOPE`, `QUARANTINED`, `REVOKED`, `RETIRED`.

## PM-PROVIDER-002 — Operation-Scoped Trust

Read/telemetry trust does not imply mutation trust.

## PM-PROVIDER-003 — Authenticated Provider Transport

Provider channels bind endpoint identity, integrity, anti-replay, confidentiality where required, Core/Provider epochs, and negotiated capability/version semantics.

## PM-PROVIDER-004 — Provider Mutation Contract

Defines Provider incarnation, target identity, fence, action correlation, execution deadline/lease, commit boundary, replay class, cancellation, and evidence semantics.

## PM-PROVIDER-005 — Provider Selection Binding

An effect authorized against Provider A does not silently execute through Provider B unless the contract explicitly proves safe equivalent failover.

## PM-PROVIDER-006 — Provider Restart Continuity

New Provider incarnation cannot claim old action completion unless durable action continuity across restart is mechanically proven.

## PM-PROVIDER-007 — Semantic Fault Containment

Reject/quarantine reused impossible identities, wrong fence/target, stale Core, regressing sequence, impossible lifecycle, contradictory accounting, and other schema-valid semantic violations.

## PM-PROVIDER-008 — Provider Cardinality Bounds

Per-Provider/source object/edge/event cardinality and work limits prevent an authenticated Provider from exhausting Host/Manager resources. Exceeding limits yields explicit PARTIAL/DEGRADED/UNKNOWN behavior.

## PM-PROVIDER-009 — Late Commit Violation

Post-lease commit is a Provider protocol violation; reconcile actual target state, degrade/quarantine as required, and never pretend no effect occurred.

## PM-PROVIDER-010 — Trust Revocation Timing

Revocation blocks future delegation. Pending uncommitted work is fenced/cancelled where supported. Committed effects are reconciled; history is qualified rather than rewritten.

## PM-PROVIDER-011 — Provider UI Isolation

Provider HTML/JS never executes inside the bridge-enabled Manager application context. Provider-specific UI extensions, if ever supported, use a separate sandbox/security domain. Untrusted artwork is decoded outside the trusted broker.

## PM-PROVIDER-012 — Provider Activation Lifecycle

Portable activation states include dormant/starting/active/stopping/failed/unavailable or equivalent. Activation is distinct from health and capability availability. Explicit operator activation/deactivation is a typed effect; automatic policy activation is a mechanical policy event.

## PM-EXEC-001 — Execution Path

Execution is a bounded ordered path such as Host → Provider → VM → guest → runtime/container → process.

## PM-EXEC-002 — Execution Layer Contract

Each layer declares authority/incarnation, placement revision, observation depth, mutation depth, accounting scope, clock domain, locator namespace, and continuity semantics.

## PM-EXEC-003 — Snapshot/Restore Incarnation

Execution layers capable of snapshot/clone/restore/fork change incarnation when continuity cannot be mechanically preserved. Old IDs/fences/cursors cannot target resurrected earlier state.

## PM-EXEC-004 — Placement Migration Continuity

Identity survives migration only when mechanically proven by the authoritative layer; otherwise new identity or UNKNOWN continuity.

## PM-EXEC-005 — Nested Mutation Correlation

One originating Host Action correlation is preserved end-to-end while each authority layer contributes its own authenticated fence/result evidence.

