# U. WORKLOAD, PROCESS, AND SERVICE MODELS

## PM-WORKLOAD-001 — Workload Identity Model

Application → Profile revision → launch/admission → Workload Launch/Attempt → optional live Workload Instance → processes.

## PM-WORKLOAD-002 — Workload Ownership

Prime Exec records workload owner/admitter explicitly. Execution UID/account is separate. Historical principal IDs are not re-resolved from current usernames.

## PM-WORKLOAD-003 — Workload Lifecycle

Portable states include admitted/starting/running/suspended/stopping/completed/failed/interrupted/lost-or-unknown or equivalent.

## PM-WORKLOAD-004 — Workload Completion

Portable completion distinguishes success/failure/termination/crash/interruption/UNKNOWN as appropriate.

## PM-WORKLOAD-005 — Workload Continuity

Backend declares continuity across Core restart, Host reboot, Provider reconnect, and migration. Continuity requires mechanical proof.

## PM-WORKLOAD-006 — Live Workload Registry

Core owns recoverable current workload truth including lifecycle, placement, Profile/Policy, process membership, Provider, runtime overrides, effective enforcement, and transition/quiescence state.

## PM-EXEC-PRIME-001 — Asynchronous Prime Exec

Prime Exec admits, assigns identities, durably records admission, returns a live identity, supervises asynchronously, emits lifecycle events, and preserves completion evidence. Launch API does not wait for process exit.

## PM-WORKLOAD-007 — Launch Provenance

Record initiating principal/client/system authority, application, exact Profile/Policy revisions, generation, runtime/backend, Provider, and upstream correlation.

## PM-PROCESS-001 — Process Identity

PID alone is insufficient. After Core restart, continuity requires boot epoch + PID + mechanically verified process-start identity and relevant domain identity. Otherwise new process identity or UNKNOWN continuity.

## PM-PROCESS-002 — Process Revision Domains

Action-relevant revisions can include lifecycle, executable/image, credentials/security, workload membership, placement, and protection-relevant state.

## PM-PROCESS-003 — exec()/Credential/Placement Safety

Relevant changes can stale an effect even if PID remains the same.

## PM-PROCESS-004 — Process Attribution

Processes may be managed workload members, Host/system, unmanaged user, Provider/runtime helper, shared infrastructure, or unattributed. Membership need not be exclusive.

## PM-PROCESS-005 — Shared Infrastructure

Shared helpers are not arbitrarily assigned or charged to one workload. Per-workload control is available only where the backend can isolate the effect.

## PM-PROCESS-006 — Ownership / Initiator / Execution Credential

These are separate concepts and authoritative sources.

## PM-PROCESS-007 — Invocation Model

Do not assume universal Unix argv. Represent authoritative native invocation, parsed/derived representation, provenance, and safe display.

## PM-PROCESS-008 — Native String / Path Model

Preserve authoritative platform representation, including arbitrary Unix bytes and Windows-native representations, separately from safe Unicode display.

## PM-PROCESS-009 — Process Tree Control

Tree effects define descendant resolution, newly created descendants, per-target authorization/protection, convergence bounds, survivor reporting, and target-set semantics. Managed workload stop is preferred where it better represents intent.

## PM-DISPLAY-001 — Safe Machine-Supplied Display

Machine-controlled strings are escaped, bounded, bidi-isolated, and never interpreted as markup. High-risk surfaces show stable identity/domain alongside display labels.

## PM-SERVICE-001 — Service Model

Separate service definition, immutable component definition revision, Host override/config revision, activation instances, workload/process relationships, Provider, and persistence kind.

## PM-SERVICE-002 — Service Target Scopes

`SERVICE_DEFINITION`, `ACTIVATION_INSTANCE`, `ALL_CURRENT_ACTIVATIONS` are distinct targets. Persistent activation configuration is separate from runtime control.

## PM-SERVICE-003 — One-Shot Semantics

Successful one-shot service may complete and become inactive.

## PM-SERVICE-004 — Supervisor Truth

Terminating one child does not imply service/workload stopped; replacement child may appear while the higher-level lifecycle remains active.

## PM-SERVICE-005 — Service CAS

Persistent service override/config changes use compare-and-swap against the observed revision; concurrent writer yields conflict.

## PM-SERVICE-006 — Activation Revision Pinning

Every activation records the definition/config revision it started from. Updating definition/override does not rewrite running activation provenance.

## PM-SERVICE-007 — Activation Continuity

Activation identity survives authority restart only when mechanically reconstructable; otherwise prior activation becomes ended/lost-or-unknown and newly observed activation gets a new identity.

## PM-SERVICE-008 — Restart Count Epoch

Displayed restart counts include their source/reset epoch.

## PM-SUSPEND-001 — Multi-Causal Suspension

Effective suspension can have multiple causes. Resume removes only the authorized cause it controls; other active causes keep the target suspended.

